{"suite":"ltp","test":"fsync03","verdict":"bug","commit":"68bdd4a99","run":"run-7UHmXC","owning_layer":"PerOpenFileOps per-open file description layer in kernel/src/fs/file/inode_handle.rs, overridden in kernel/src/fs/pipe/common.rs","summary":"sys_fsync gates only on the InodeHandle downcast, but pipes and fifos are InodeHandles in Asterinas, so the no-op default Inode::sync_all returns Ok(0) where Linux returns EINVAL; fdatasync shares the defect"}
{"suite":"ltp","test":"dup02","verdict":"already-green","commit":"68bdd4a99","run":"run-atDPcd","owning_layer":null,"summary":"dup02 passes as-is (2/2, matches host LTP 20260529): negative oldfd is rejected by TryFrom<RawFileDesc> for FileDesc and unopened fd 1500 by FileTable::duplicate_entry, both EBADF before any fd allocation; only the all.txt pool entry (line 160) is stale"}
{"suite":"ltp","test":"clock_getres01","verdict":"bug","commit":"e8f2b9ca6","run":"run-iIbwju","owning_layer":"POSIX clock layer in kernel/src/time (Clock trait in time/core/mod.rs + the clockid_t dispatch read_clock owns in syscall/clock_gettime.rs)","summary":"clock_getres is absent from both syscall tables (x86.rs skips 229, generic.rs skips 114) so it returns ENOSYS; separately vdso.rs hrtimer_res is initialised to 0 and never written, so the prebuilt Linux vDSO reports 0ns resolution for REALTIME/MONOTONIC/MONOTONIC_RAW/BOOTTIME today. Result 12 pass/10 fail/2 skip vs host 44/0/0; the 2 skips are tst_syscall turning ENOSYS into tst_brk(TCONF), aborting both raw-syscall variants"}
{"suite":"ltp","test":"epoll_ctl02","verdict":"bug","commit":"e8f2b9ca6","run":"run-rVlQYu","owning_layer":"epoll_ctl entry path (kernel/src/syscall/epoll.rs sys_epoll_ctl + top of EpollFile::control) for the self-add EINVAL; per-open file description layer (FileLike/PerOpenFileOps pollability predicate, InodeHandle at kernel/src/fs/file/inode_handle.rs:251-263) for the EPERM","summary":"7/9 pass; 2 fail because epoll_ctl returns 0 instead of erroring. No pollability predicate exists (Pollable::poll is a required FileLike method, so there is no null f_op->poll to test) and InodeHandle::poll returns IN|OUT unconditionally, so a non-pollable fd registers instead of EPERM; and nothing compares epfd against the target file, so an epoll file can be added to itself instead of EINVAL. Host probe: Linux EPERMs regular files and /dev/null too, not just dirs, and the self-add check is struct file identity so dup(epfd) is also rejected; poll() must still report 0x5 on those files"}
{"suite":"ltp","test":"timerfd_gettime01","verdict":"bug","commit":"e8f2b9ca6","run":"run-f6fYXT","owning_layer":"syscall entry layer: argument-validation order in kernel/src/syscall/timerfd_gettime.rs","summary":"A hand-rolled NULL guard at lines 17-19 returns EINVAL before the fd is ever resolved, so fd=-1 with curr_value=NULL reports EINVAL instead of EBADF (2/3 pass vs host 3/3). The guard is redundant: write_val already yields EFAULT for address 0 via check_vaddr_lowerbound, and downcast_ref::<TimerfdFile>() already yields EINVAL for a non-timerfd. Fix is to delete lines 17-19. Same antipattern in sys_timer_gettime (timer_settime.rs:69) and sys_getitimer (setitimer.rs:85)."}
{"suite":"gvisor","test":"readv_test","case":"ReadvTest.BadIovecBase_File:ReadvTest.BadIovecBase_Pipe:ReadvTest.IovecOutsideTaskAddressRangeInNonemptyArray","verdict":"bug","commit":"e8f2b9ca6","run":"run-qMdgxS","owning_layer":"iovec import in kernel/src/util/iovec.rs copy_iovs_and_convert (above all FileLike and socket callers, = Linux __import_iovec)","summary":"0/3 pass vs host 3/3. IoVec::is_empty (line 47) treats base==0 as empty and the loop continues at 108-110 before any base validation, so {NULL,0x400} is dropped and readv returns 0 instead of faulting to EFAULT, and {usize::MAX,0} is skipped unchecked so iov[1] still copies 284. Linux checks access_ok per entry in __import_iovec regardless of len, and access_ok never rejects NULL (fault comes at copy time). Six callers share the defect incl. writev/sendmsg/recvmsg. Must keep NullIovecInNonemptyArray ({0,0} skipped, returns 284) green"}
{"suite":"ltp","test":"socket01","verdict":"bug","commit":"e8f2b9ca6","run":"run-STzXZ3","owning_layer":"per-family socket-creation dispatch (Linux net_proto_family::create); Asterinas has none, sys_socket inlines the whole table in kernel/src/syscall/socket.rs and socketpair.rs keeps a divergent copy","summary":"5/9 pass vs host 9/9. Every rejection arm returns EAFNOSUPPORT whichever argument was wrong: lines 50/60 say EAFNOSUPPORT while their message says unsupported protocol (tdat[5] proto17, tdat[6] proto6, tdat[8] proto1), and AF_INET+SOCK_RAW has no arm so tdat[3] reaches the domain catch-all at line 93. EPROTONOSUPPORT/ESOCKTNOSUPPORT are defined at error.rs:110-111 and used nowhere in kernel/src. Trap: line 93 must keep EAFNOSUPPORT because AF_UNSPEC=0 is a real variant and tdat[0] passes through it. SOCK_RAW+proto0 is EPROTONOSUPPORT even as root, never EPERM. Same defect in socketpair.rs:51 and the netlink arms 74-86"}
{"suite":"ltp","test":"renameat202","verdict":"bug","commit":"e8f2b9ca6","run":"run-ps0EP2","owning_layer":"ext2 on-disk directory-entry layer: Inode::rename in kernel/src/fs/fs_impls/ext2/inode/dir/mod.rs:233, reached via ext2/impl_for_vfs/inode.rs:213","summary":"0/1 pass vs host 10/10. ext2/impl_for_vfs/inode.rs:220-222 unconditionally returns EINVAL for RenameMode::Exchange before any work, so renameat2(RENAME_EXCHANGE) fails at the syscall return. VFS is already Exchange-complete (dentry.rs:762-893 has both swap arms, check_rename_mode gives ENOENT) and ramfs implements it (fs.rs:1152,1191), so the gap is ext2-only. Guard is not merely redundant: ext2 dir rename takes no mode, and apply_dir_mutations (dir/mod.rs:329-408) overwrite-then-deletes and drops the replaced inode link count / remove_inode at 382-394, so deleting the guard alone would destroy the destination instead of swapping. Needs an Exchange branch doing two set_entry_target() calls (helper exists at dir/mod.rs:725) and skipping step 4.2 plus the Replace-only ENOTDIR/EISDIR/ENOTEMPTY checks at 309-324. runtest is -i 10 so the swap alternates direction. renameat201 shares the cause; exfat.txt:54 and virtiofs stay out of scope"}
{"suite":"ltp","test":"setpriority02","verdict":"bug","commit":"e8f2b9ca6","run":"run-XCbdtE","owning_layer":"process-credential permission layer, beside check_signal_perm in kernel/src/process/kill.rs:161 and check_rlimit_perm in syscall/prlimit64.rs:97","summary":"6/7 pass vs host 7/7. sys_set_priority (set_priority.rs:13-45) has no ownership check at all - no euid/ruid/EPERM/CapSet in the file - so an unprivileged caller targeting pid 1 falls through to the RLIMIT_NICE arm at line 36 and gets EACCES instead of EPERM. RLIMIT_NICE is 0 for every process (rlimit.rs:20,69) so any negative prio trips it. Linux set_one_prio_perm (kernel/sys.c) checks euid vs target uid/euid then CAP_SYS_NICE and maps false to EPERM strictly before the can_nice EACCES arm. CapSet::SYS_NICE exists (capabilities.rs:34) unused. Must apply per process inside the get_processes loop since PRIO_PGRP/PRIO_USER fan out. Trap: subtests 5+6 (which=0/1, who=0) must stay EACCES - target is the caller itself. sys_nice is unimplemented so no other caller of this path; sched_setattr/setscheduler/setparam via access_sched_attr_with and ioprio_set share the gap but are out of scope. setpriority01 needs the FIXME at lines 38-40, out of scope"}
{"suite":"ltp","test":"llseek01","verdict":"bug","commit":"e8f2b9ca6","run":"run-IsX1sM","owning_layer":"per-open file description layer: InodeHandle::write/write_at in kernel/src/fs/file/inode_handle.rs:287-342 (= Linux generic_write_checks, VFS-side)","summary":"1 pass/1 fail vs host 5/5. RLIMIT_FSIZE is never consulted on any write path - grep FSIZE in kernel/src/ hits only truncate.rs:56 and fallocate.rs:55, zero hits in kernel/src/fs/. write() at offset 655360 with limit 16384 returns 8192 instead of EFBIG+SIGXFSZ, so llseek01.c:39 tst_brk aborts before assertions 3-5. Linux generic_write_check_limits (fs/read_write.c) tests pos>=limit on the START offset, send_sig(SIGXFSZ) then -EFBIG, else clamps count to limit-pos as a silent short write. Traps: assertion 4 must SUCCEED in full at offset 8192 landing exactly on 16384, so never compare offset+len; under-limit case is a short write with no signal; pipes must stay unaffected (they exit at the !is_offset_aware early return) to keep fsync03 green. Same defect in pwrite64.rs:35, pwritev.rs:114+161, sendfile.rs:159 - all funnel through inode_handle so one check fixes five syscalls. SIGXFSZ already has SigDefaultAction::Core; copy the enqueue shape from pipe/common.rs:478-487"}
{"suite":"ltp","test":"brk02","verdict":"bug","commit":"e8f2b9ca6","run":"run-lycyHn","owning_layer":"Process heap-management layer (Heap::modify_heap_end in kernel/src/process/process_vm/heap.rs)","summary":"check_single_mapping=true at heap.rs:143 requires the heap to be one VmMapping; mprotect splits it so check_lies_in_single_mapping returns EFAULT, brk returns the unchanged break instead of expanding/shrinking across the split, failing brk02.c:75 (3 failures: both variants fail the expand-after-mprotect assertion, libc variant adds a TST_EXP_PASS_SILENT TFAIL from the glibc wrapper detecting newbrk < requested)"}
{"suite":"ltp","test":"mkdir02","verdict":"bug","commit":"e8f2b9ca6","run":"run-YIMPAV","owning_layer":"VFS inode-creation layer: DirDentry::create/mknod in kernel/src/fs/vfs/path/dentry.rs:399-414,606-622","summary":"0/2 pass vs host 1/1. S_ISGID inheritance is absent from all of kernel/src/fs/ - grep S_ISGID|has_set_gid finds one non-definition use (hardlink CAP_FOWNER check, vfs/path/mod.rs:306-308). ramfs create takes gid from current_fs_ids()=fsgid only (ramfs/fs.rs:945,1453-1461) and passes mode through, though self IS the parent; ext2 duplicates it (ext2/fs.rs:394-406, ignores its parent_ino). So a dir made in an S_ISGID parent gets gid 65534 (creator nobody) + mode 0777 instead of the parent gid 2 + S_ISGID. Ruled out chmod/chown losing the bit on the parent: ramfs set_mode stores mode verbatim (855-860). Rule missing for open(O_CREAT)/mknod/symlink/O_TMPFILE/AF_UNIX bind too, and in 6 filesystems, so fix above the fs not in it. Traps: non-dirs inherit gid but NOT the bit, requested S_ISGID stripped for non-member creator, plain parent keeps fsgid, inheritance has no perm check"}
{"suite":"ltp","test":"semget02","verdict":"bug","commit":"e8f2b9ca6","run":"run-n8fKcM","owning_layer":"System V IPC permission layer: IpcPermission in kernel/src/ipc/mod.rs, invoked from IpcNamespace::validate_sem_set in ipc_ns.rs (= Linux ipcperms in ipc/util.c)","summary":"IpcNamespace::validate_sem_set (kernel/src/ipc/ipc_ns.rs:177-184) is a stub that returns Ok unconditionally, so no SysV IPC permission check exists anywhere. subtest 1 (semget from nobody uid on a root-owned mode-0 set) succeeded instead of EACCES. Five other subtests pass (EEXIST/ENOENT/EINVAL). The semaphore-set data is correct; validate_sem_set merely never reads it. Also line 145 hardcodes ALTER|READ instead of deriving from semflg as Linux does, and check order is perms-EEXIST-nsems where Linux does EEXIST-nsems-perms. All semctl and semop calls also reach the stub so any process can read/write any set"}
{"suite":"ltp","test":"timer_create01","verdict":"bug","commit":"e8f2b9ca6","run":"run-nQNo7j","owning_layer":"sigevent dispatch in kernel/src/syscall/timer_create.rs (syscall entry)","summary":"SIGEV_THREAD match arm is a bare unimplemented!() at timer_create.rs:74, so a valid sigev_notify=2 from unprivileged userspace panics and halts the whole kernel; Linux treats SIGEV_THREAD as SIGEV_SIGNAL (thread-group enqueue, thread spawning is glibc-side), so the arm needs the SIGEV_SIGNAL enqueue logic. Probe fix gave passed 25 / skipped 15 / failed 0."}
{"suite":"ltp","test":"personality01","verdict":"already-green","commit":"e8f2b9ca6","run":"run-KgPVlh","owning_layer":null,"summary":"personality01 passes as-is (19/19, matches host LTP 20260529): sys_personality already returns the pre-call value on every path and treats 0xffffffff as a pure query (personality.rs:9-12,25), and stores the argument verbatim so flag bits above PER_MASK round-trip; syscall wired on x86 (135) and generic (92). Only the all.txt pool entry (line 1051) is stale. Out of scope but real: personality is not inherited across fork/clone - builder.rs:208 hardcodes AtomicU32::new(0) and no clone site copies it (the FIXME at personality.rs:7-8); host probe confirms Linux does inherit it, but this test cannot see that since each child sets its own value before querying"}
{"suite":"ltp","test":"timer_gettime01","verdict":"bug","commit":"e8f2b9ca6","run":"run-5G5q5b","owning_layer":"syscall entry in kernel/src/syscall/timer_settime.rs","summary":"timer_gettime pre-checks itimerspec_addr==0 returning EINVAL; should let write_val return EFAULT naturally after timer_id validation"}
{"suite":"ltp","test":"open04","verdict":"bug","commit":"e8f2b9ca6","run":"run-ubLFfF","owning_layer":"File descriptor allocator: FileTable::insert and FileTable::dup_ceil in kernel/src/fs/file/file_table.rs","summary":"FileTable::insert (line 190) and dup_ceil (line 139) never consult RLIMIT_NOFILE. open(2) succeeds beyond the limit instead of returning EMFILE. The check is entirely absent—affects every fd-allocating syscall (open, dup, pipe, socket, accept, etc). Linux enforces this in __alloc_fd (fs/file.c) before allocation."}
{"suite":"ltp","test":"alarm02","verdict":"already-green","commit":"e8f2b9ca6","run":"run-FAFjvy","owning_layer":null,"summary":"test passes completely with all 6 assertions (alarm with large timeout values), pool entry is stale"}
{"suite":"ltp","test":"capset02","verdict":"already-green","commit":"e8f2b9ca6","run":"run-il8HB1","owning_layer":null,"summary":"all 6 error cases pass: EFAULT for bad addresses, EINVAL for bad version, EPERM for capability violations"}
{"suite":"ltp","test":"chroot01","verdict":"already-green","commit":"e8f2b9ca6","run":"run-pt50FW","owning_layer":null,"summary":"Test passes in Asterinas - unprivileged chroot() correctly returns EPERM"}
{"suite":"ltp","test":"chmod01","verdict":"bug","commit":"e8f2b9ca6","run":"run-UU0hHd","owning_layer":"Filesystem inode operations (exfat set_mode)","summary":"set_mode() returns Ok(()) without changing permissions, causing chmod to succeed silently while stat shows unchanged mode"}
{"suite":"ltp","test":"bind02","verdict":"already-green","commit":"e8f2b9ca6","run":"run-K7IxzT","owning_layer":null,"summary":"test passes in Asterinas; pool entry in all.txt is stale"}
{"suite":"ltp","test":"prctl05","verdict":"bug","commit":"e8f2b9ca6","run":"run-Du6S4W","owning_layer":"PR_SET_NAME arm of sys_prctl in kernel/src/syscall/prctl.rs:65-71 (syscall-entry arg marshalling); truncation stays in ThreadName::set_name_as_bytes","summary":"4 pass/1 fail vs host 8/8. prctl.rs:68 reads the name with read_cstring(addr,MAX_THREAD_NAME_LEN=16), the reject-on-overflow reader, so an 18-byte name gets ENAMETOOLONG (36) at prctl05.c:45 instead of being silently truncated to 15 bytes. Linux prctl uses strncpy_from_user(count=15) and only faults. ThreadName::set_name_as_bytes (name.rs:29-33) already clamps to 15 correctly and is never reached. Fix: use read_cstring_until_end (util/read_cstring.rs:57) via the public user_space().reader(). Do NOT change read_cstring itself - ENAMETOOLONG is right for its other 51 path/xattr/memfd callers. Only user-space name setter; /proc comm write is EOPNOTSUPP"}
{"suite":"ltp","test":"brk01","verdict":"already-green","commit":"e8f2b9ca6","run":"run-S7ARh2","owning_layer":null,"summary":"brk() syscall works correctly, test passes in both libc and syscall variants"}
{"suite":"ltp","test":"madvise01","verdict":"already-green","commit":"e8f2b9ca6","run":"run-INnRcf","owning_layer":null,"summary":"passes as-is: 10 TPASS + 10 TCONF, 0 failed/broken/warnings. The 9 DUMMY_MADVISE advices plus MADV_DONTNEED return 0; the other 10 (REMOVE, DONTFORK, DOFORK, HWPOISON, DONTDUMP, DODUMP, WIPEONFORK, KEEPONFORK, COLD, PAGEOUT) return EINVAL at madvise.rs:45, which madvise01.c:104 treats as TCONF not failure. Host is 20/20. Only the all.txt line 1031 pool entry is stale; real gap in those 10 advices remains but this test tolerates it by design"}
{"suite":"ltp","test":"memfd_create01","verdict":"already-green","commit":"e8f2b9ca6","run":"run-ifrhdN","owning_layer":null,"summary":"passes as-is (157/157, exactly matches host LTP 20260529 with 0 failed/broken/skipped). All 9 seal subtests are covered by the dedicated MemfdInode in kernel/src/fs/fs_impls/ramfs/memfd.rs: seals held in a Mutex on the inode so dup and /proc/self/fd reopens share them, F_SEAL_SEAL pre-seeded when MFD_ALLOW_SEALING is absent, EPERM without Rights::WRITE, EBUSY via page_cache writable_mapping_status().deny() under a live writable mapping, SHRINK/GROW enforced in resize, and Linux EOF-page granularity for growing writes. Only the all.txt line 1879 pool entry is stale; fix is the one-line uncomment, no kernel change. memfd_create02/03/04 (lines 1880-1882) are still commented and were not probed"}
{"suite":"ltp","test":"wait402","verdict":"bug","commit":"e8f2b9ca6","run":"run-dCeGov","owning_layer":"PID allocation policy: PID_MAX const in kernel/src/process/posix_thread/mod.rs:425","summary":"PID_MAX = u32::MAX/2 = i32::MAX exactly, and procfs/sys/kernel/pid_max.rs:29 prints it verbatim, so pid_max+1 overflows the signed pid_t to i32::MIN; ProcessFilter::from_id (process_filter.rs:53-55) then returns ESRCH instead of reaching the correct ECHILD at wait.rs:81-86. wait4 logic is CORRECT and matches Linux kernel_wait4 (which also ESRCHes INT_MIN, confirmed by host probe) - do NOT touch process_filter.rs. Fix is PID_MAX -> 4194304 (Linux PID_MAX_LIMIT). Host 1/1 ECHILD with pid_max 4194304. Same ceiling also wrongly bounds cgroupfs pids.max (pids.rs:114) and is shared by sys_kill."}
{"suite":"ltp","test":"times03","verdict":"missing-feature","commit":"e8f2b9ca6","run":"run-Vr5gET","owning_layer":"new sys_times handler at the syscall-entry layer: kernel/src/syscall/times.rs + SYS_TIMES=100 in arch/x86.rs, 153 in arch/generic.rs","summary":"sys_times is entirely unimplemented (absent from both dispatch tables), so number 100 hits the ENOSYS fallback at syscall/mod.rs:337 and glibc, which flags failure only on EFAULT, does not report it - times03 reads uninitialized stack garbage (buf1.tms_utime=206158430240) and fails 8/12 vs host 12/12. Additive fix only: process ProfClock user/kernel clocks + reaped_children_stats already supply all four fields (same sources as getrusage.rs:26-52), must convert 1000Hz jiffies down to USER_HZ=100 ticks"}
{"suite":"ltp","test":"getsockopt01","verdict":"missing-feature","commit":"e8f2b9ca6","run":"run-OgXQbF","owning_layer":null,"summary":"bind(0.0.0.0) unsupported: get_iface_to_bind (net/socket/ip/common.rs:17-34) matches ifaces by exact addr equality, so INADDR_ANY hits the EADDRNOTAVAIL arm at common.rs:88-93 and setup() TBROKs before any getsockopt assertion runs; needs netns-scoped socket table + port-keyed listener hashtable (aster-bigtcp/src/socket_table.rs:44-54). Blocks 23 INADDR_ANY LTP tests. getsockopt.rs is NOT on the failing path."}
{"suite":"ltp","test":"setitimer01","verdict":"bug","commit":"e8f2b9ca6","run":"run-UceFMi","owning_layer":"PosixTimerManager in kernel/src/process/process/timer_manager.rs (create_process_timer_callback factory, called at line 132)","summary":"16 pass/2 fail vs host 18/18, both failures at setitimer01.c:124 killed by SIGALRM. create_process_timer_callback (timer_manager.rs:106-126) hardcodes KernelSignal::new(SIGALRM) at line 111 and takes no signal param, and its single WorkItem is cloned into all three timers at 134-138, so ITIMER_VIRTUAL and ITIMER_PROF enqueue signal 14 instead of SIGVTALRM(26)/SIGPROF(27). Clocks, arming, interval re-arm and ovalue readback are all already correct; ITIMER_REAL passes. SIGVTALRM/SIGPROF exist (constants.rs:55-56) but have zero senders in kernel/src and already default to Term. Fix: add a SigNum param and call the factory once per timer. One caller in tree; alarm.rs:14 and procfs stat.rs:201 need alarm_timer to keep SIGALRM"}
